      "has_more": false,
      "messages": [
        {
          "content": "Juror-2 check of the September 2026 bank reconciliation for account ending 4402. Adjusted bank balance = 18,642.35 + 2,150.00 deposit in transit - 480.00 - 1,215.40 - 96.25 outstanding cheques = 19,000.70. Adjusted book balance = 19,290.70 cash book - 40.00 bank charges = 19,250.70. The two do not agree: the adjusted book balance exceeds the adjusted bank balance by exactly 250.00. So the claim that the adjusted book balance equals the adjusted bank balance does not hold. The preparer's note 'Reconciled, no differences' is an assertion rather than a computation, and it is contradicted by the figures on the same page. The individual lines are each arithmetically sound, so this is not a case of a mistyped subtotal: the reconciliation simply does not balance, and the document states that no other reconciling items were identified, so the 250.00 is unexplained on the record. Uncertainties: this reconciliation is the only document in the case, the cash book is stated to be closed for September, and I am applying the standard form of reconciliation the document itself sets out (bank statement plus deposits in transit less outstanding cheques; cash book less unrecorded bank charges). A further reconciling item of 250.00 would close the gap, but none is identified. On the record as submitted the claim does not hold.",
          "content_hash": "0x61aa83524b582d98a04cdc1aa2b3caa54e9b6b5fa2706e0173425dcbbaf6867f",
          "created_at_ms": 1793322978554,
          "message_id": "98c24778-ec89-4ff0-b04c-a791d4d2c2e7",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-2",
          "sequence": 1
        },
        {
          "content": "Challenged. The September reconciliation does not show equal adjusted balances. Adjusted bank balance: 18,642.35 + 2,150.00 deposit in transit - 480.00 (cheque 1043) - 1,215.40 (cheque 1047) - 96.25 (cheque 1049) = 19,000.70. Adjusted book balance: 19,290.70 - 40.00 bank charges not yet entered = 19,250.70. They differ by 250.00, so the claim that they are equal does not hold. The preparer's note 'Reconciled, no differences' and the statement that no other reconciling items were identified do not close the gap: they assert a conclusion the printed arithmetic contradicts, and an unreconciled 250.00 is exactly the item that would have been listed had it been found. To agree, the book side would need 19,000.70 (cash book 19,040.70) or the bank side +250.00 more; neither appears in the schedule. For completeness, the unadjusted figures differ by 648.35, and the listed items explain only 358.35 on the bank side and 40.00 on the book side, leaving the 250.00. Uncertainty: this schedule is the only document in the case, so the individual figures are taken as printed - the defect is in the printed arithmetic, not in missing paperwork.",
          "content_hash": "0x64d2dae7441f3f536df291062045277109f91a79581f40ff4d7416c6ebfaee52",
          "created_at_ms": 1793322983569,
          "message_id": "ee0dd48b-f196-4c2d-984f-8fec09f98790",
          "round_num": null,
          "sender_id": "challenger",
          "sequence": 2
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror-1 independent check of the September 2026 bank reconciliation for account ending 4402. Adjusted bank balance: 18,642.35 statement + 2,150.00 deposit in transit - 480.00 (cheque 1043) - 1,215.40 (cheque 1047) - 96.25 (cheque 1049) = 19,000.70. Adjusted book balance: 19,290.70 cash book - 40.00 bank charges not yet entered = 19,250.70. These are not equal: the adjusted book balance is 250.00 higher than the adjusted bank balance. So the claim as published does not hold on the document's own figures. The preparer's note 'Reconciled, no differences' is a conclusion, not an arithmetic step, and it is contradicted by the numbers on the same page; the statement that no other reconciling items were identified means the 250.00 is unexplained on this record rather than resolved by something not shown. Cross-checks: the unadjusted balances differ by 648.35 (19,290.70 - 18,642.35); the listed items account for 1,791.65 of cheques and a 2,150.00 transit deposit on the bank side and 40.00 of charges on the book side, and the residual is exactly the 250.00 - so this is a genuine unreconciled difference, not a mistyped subtotal or an omitted line I have double-counted. For the two sides to agree, either the cash book would need to be 19,040.70 or the bank side would need a further 250.00 credit, and neither appears. Uncertainties: this schedule is the only document in the case and the figures are taken as printed; an additional reconciling item of 250.00 would close the gap, but the document states none was identified and the cash book is closed for September. On the record as submitted the claim does not hold.",
          "content_hash": "0x53557316c732832e9b3fe58035b6a5e4a1dae0c5c8b647b162faf10a51c8df33",
          "created_at_ms": 1793323028884,
          "message_id": "da529740-d05a-43b2-9ef5-a19166c85df3",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-1",
          "sequence": 3
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror-3 check of the September 2026 bank reconciliation (Hollis Facilities Management, account ending 4402). Computing both adjusted balances exactly as the schedule presents them. Bank side: 18,642.35 + 2,150.00 deposit in transit - 480.00 cheque 1043 - 1,215.40 cheque 1047 - 96.25 cheque 1049 = 19,000.70. Book side: 19,290.70 - 40.00 bank charges = 19,250.70. The two adjusted balances differ by 250.00, so the claim that they are equal does not hold. I re-added each side in a different order to be sure this is not a slip: the outstanding cheques total 1,791.65, and 18,642.35 + 2,150.00 - 1,791.65 = 19,000.70; the book side is a single deduction, 19,290.70 - 40.00 = 19,250.70. The gap is exactly 250.00, which is the kind of round figure that usually points to a missing reconciling item rather than a summation error, but the schedule states that no other reconciling items were identified and that the cash book is closed for September, so no such item can be supplied from this record. Two things that could mislead a reader. First, the preparer's note says 'Reconciled, no differences', which is contradicted by the schedule's own figures and is not evidence of agreement; the arithmetic governs, not the note. Second, the unadjusted difference of 648.35 (19,290.70 - 18,642.35) is not the test, and neither is any pairing that omits one of the listed items: dropping the deposit in transit gives 16,850.70, and ignoring the bank charges gives 19,290.70, and neither of those equals the other side either. The comparison the claim names is adjusted book against adjusted bank, and on that comparison the answer is 19,250.70 against 19,000.70. Uncertainties: this reconciliation is the only document in the case; if a 250.00 item exists that the preparer failed to list, it would close the gap, but the document expressly says none was identified and the cash book is closed, so on this record the difference stands unexplained. The discrepancy on the face of the document is not in doubt, only its cause. On the record as submitted the claim does not hold.",
          "content_hash": "0x649bc38827f50acf400ef4bc2f457f42e8e731b6e783cfabb1e72d446f480c2d",
          "created_at_ms": 1793323034319,
          "message_id": "87dfe880-da1f-48f8-b405-338a3d8f4460",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-3",
          "sequence": 4
        }
      ],
